== ROBIN ==

Small desk calculator, inspired by Ivy.
Rust.

Rules:
1. No operator precedence, right-associative ops.
2. Results are exact and use rational arithmetic, but some functions are irrational, which uses high-precision floats.
3. Whitespace separates tokens; it has no other meaning.

Whitespace:
Tokens scanned by maximal munch.
Whitespace is required between tokens that would merge:
2e5     A number
2 e5    A number + identifier `e5`
1/3     A rational
1 / 3   A number + division op
Newlines end an expression.
Adjacent operands with no operator are an error.
A rational literal is one token, an atom, so spacing changes meaning
where an op follows:
1/2/3       == (1 div 2) div 3 == 1/6
1 / 2 / 3   == 1 div (2 div 3) == 3/2
Accepted: same tradeoff as 2e5 vs 2 e5.

Syntax:
Unary (abs x) and infix binary ops (x pow y) only.
Sugar wrappers: x mul y == x * y
No precedence. 2*3+4 = 14, not 10
Brackets to override. (2*3)+4 = 10

Numeric tower:
Int         i64
BigInt
BigRat      stored reduced
BigFloat    inexact. sqrt, log, exp, etc.

Promotion:
Every binary op (a op b) lifts a and b to max(a, b) for the computation,
the result takes the form it needs, and then may be demoted.
(3.5 mul 2) -> (7/2 mul 2/1) -> 7/1 -> 7
(1 div 3)   -> 1/3
(4 div 2)   -> 2/1 -> 2
(2 mul pi)  -> (2.0 mul pi) -> 6.28...

Demotion:
BigInt -> Int       OK, iff it fits
BigRat -> BigInt    OK, iff denom == 1      3/1 == 3
BigFloat -> *       NO

Equality:
Promotion only. 1 == 1.0 == 1/1
No boolean type just 0/1

Exactness:
BigFloat is a one-way operation.
Can only be produced with
    sqrt, log, exp
    trig + hyperbolic + inverse fns
    pow with non-integer exponent
    pi, euler
    float (conversion op)
    promotion
Exact is tried first. sqrt 4 -> 2.
Decimal literals are exact. 1.234 = 1234/1000, reduced to 617/500
2e5 == 200000
1e-3 == 1/1000

Floats use 256 bits of mantissa by default.

Literals:
Self-delimiting.
999         Base 10
0x30        Base 16
0b10101     Base 2
2e5         exponent
1.234       decimal
1/3         rational (int/int, no spaces)
1j2         reserved for future

Only int/int merges into a rational literal; a decimal or exponent
numerator does not. 1.5/2 and 2e5/3 are division.

Printing:
Rationals print as n/d, reduced, sign on the numerator: -1/3.
denom == 1 demotes, so integers print bare.
Only int/int is ever printed, which is exactly what the literal
form accepts, so output re-reads as the same value.

- can be negation and subtraction.

Errors:
No NaN, just errors.
Explicitly errors:
    sqrt of a negative (no complex)
    log 0
    0 pow 0
    div by zero, including the literal 1/0

Out of scope:
Lists
Matrix
Complex

